The assertion check states that the result of the function sum when it is called with unchanged x and incremented y should yield the current value of x as expected. This is generally what 'assertions' are for - to ensure code is behaving as expected, in production. They can be turned off with '-O' option for Python interpreter.

If you really want to find comments with "@@@" in it and generate C# Assertions out of it, that's a bit complicated as it involves parsing, so I would recommend to use some kind of linter or parser for generating such assertions.--------------------This solution requires understanding of programming, how C# works and a good grasp of Assertion statements. Here we write a basic parser for C# code for an "ArrayList" class that extracts a comment starting with "@@@".

After running a regex expression to look for the required pattern `// @@@ (.*)`, it will parse this extracted comment and replies with an Assert string in the correct format.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

namespace ListConverter
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            string code = @"
```code
public static int Sum(int x, int y)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < y; i++)
    {
        x++;
    }
    // @@@ Value of x increases by y in the function
    return x;
}
```";

            ConvertCommentsToAssert(code);

            code = File.ReadAllText("ArrayList.cs"); // read file if needed

            ConvertCommentsToAssert(code);
        }

        static void ConvertCommentsToAssert(string code)
        {
            var matches = Regex.Matches(code, @"// @@@ (.*)");
            foreach (Match match in matches)
            {
                string assert = Environment.NewLine + " Debug.Assert(" + match.Groups[1] + ");" + Environment.NewLine;
                Console.Write(match.Groups[1] + assert);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Note: This is a console program that reads the code either from a variable or a .cs file. You would need to adapt this approach to whatever frameworks you are using.

This code will first find the commented lines that start with `// @@@` and then reformats the comments in Assert format. However, this code assumes that every comment that starts with `// @@@` actually represents a valid assertion and that comments are directly below code.

Remember that not all comment markers are "//" and this code cannot handle nested blocks of comments properly or recognize special comment blocks (like XML comments).--------------------Here are the assert statement corresponds to the comment that start with "@@@" in the provided csharp code:

